{0 Optimistic changes}

A page feels fast when it shows a change at once, before the server confirms
it. {!Silica.Optimistic} holds the data for this: the last value that the
server confirmed, and the changes that wait for the server. It is a pure
value with no functions, so a machine keeps it in the data of a state and the
checker can compare it.

{1:make Make the module}

Give {!Silica.Optimistic.Make} the type of the value, the type of a change,
and how a change goes on a value. Here the value is a list of tags and a
change adds or removes one tag. [apply] is idempotent: a change that is
already on the value changes nothing (see {!section-live}):

{@ocaml[
# type change = Add of string | Remove of string;;
type change = Add of string | Remove of string
# module Tags = Silica.Optimistic.Make (struct
    type value = string list
    type nonrec change = change
    let apply tags = function
      | Add tag -> if List.mem tag tags then tags else tags @ [ tag ]
      | Remove tag -> List.filter (fun t -> t <> tag) tags
  end);;
module Tags :
  sig
    type value = string list
    type change = change/3
    type t = (value, change) Silica.Optimistic.state
    val make : value -> t
    val apply : id:int -> change -> t -> t
    val settle : ?confirmed:value -> id:int -> t -> t
    val drop : id:int -> t -> t
    val rebase : value -> t -> t
    val value : t -> value
    val confirmed : t -> value
    val pending : t -> (int * change) list
    val is_pending : id:int -> t -> bool
  end
]}

{1:steps The steps of a change}

Each change has an [id], which the app chooses, such as a counter in the
snapshot.

{t
| When | Call |
|------|------|
| the change is sent | [apply ~id change] |
| the server confirms it | [settle ~id], or [settle ~confirmed ~id] with the value of the server |
| the call fails or is cancelled | [drop ~id] |
| a new confirmed value arrives, such as a Live state | [rebase value] |
}

[value] is the confirmed value with the pending changes on it, oldest first.
The view shows [value]. [confirmed] is the value with no pending change.
[pending] lists the pending changes with their ids, oldest first.

{@ocaml[
# let tags = Tags.make [ "bug" ];;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# let tags = tags |> Tags.apply ~id:1 (Add "ui") |> Tags.apply ~id:2 (Remove "bug");;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# Tags.value tags, Tags.confirmed tags, Tags.pending tags;;
- : Tags.value * Tags.value * (int * change) list =
(["ui"], ["bug"], [(1, Add "ui"); (2, Remove "bug")])
]}

The server confirms change 1, and change 2 fails. The value goes back for
change 2 only:

{@ocaml[
# let tags = tags |> Tags.settle ~id:1 |> Tags.drop ~id:2;;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# Tags.value tags, Tags.pending tags;;
- : Tags.value * (int * change) list = (["bug"; "ui"], [])
]}

A newer value from the server keeps the changes that still wait:

{@ocaml[
# let tags = Tags.make [ "bug" ] |> Tags.apply ~id:3 (Add "ui");;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# Tags.value (Tags.rebase [ "bug"; "docs" ] tags);;
- : Tags.value = ["bug"; "docs"; "ui"]
]}

{1:machines In a machine}

For a Remote change: [apply] when the call starts, [settle] when the answer
arrives and [drop] when it fails. The calls are the pending changes: [during]
makes one Remote call for each, so a change stays pending exactly while its
call runs:

{@ocaml[
# type event = Answered of int * (unit, string) Silica.Remote.answer;;
type event = Answered of int * (unit, string) Silica.Remote.answer
# let change_tags =
    Silica.Remote.declare ~path:"/api/tags"
      ~input:
        (Silica.Codec.make
           ~encode:(function
             | Add tag -> Silica.Codec.constructor "Add" [ Silica.Codec.string.encode tag ]
             | Remove tag -> Silica.Codec.constructor "Remove" [ Silica.Codec.string.encode tag ])
           ~decode:(fun _ -> Error "not read here"))
      ~output:Silica.Codec.unit ~rejected:Silica.Codec.string;;
val change_tags : (change, unit, string) Silica.Remote.endpoint = <abstr>
# let during tags =
    List.map
      (fun (id, change) ->
        Silica.Remote.call change_tags change (fun answer -> Answered (id, answer)))
      (Tags.pending tags);;
val during : Tags.t -> event Silica.Host.t list = <fun>
]}

{2:live Live data}

Experimental: Live updates can change. The checker and the runtime do not
agree yet on the order of the first update of a subscription.

For data on a Live topic, the changes still go to the server by Remote calls:
[apply], [settle] and [drop] as above, and [rebase] for each state of the
topic. The server publishes the new state as soon as it has made the change,
so a state that already holds the change can arrive before the answer to its
call. The change is then in the confirmed value and still pending, and
[value] applies it once more. So [apply] must be idempotent: applying a change
that is already on the value gives the same value. The [Add] of [Tags] checks
that the tag is not there yet; with [tags @ [ tag ]] alone the tag would show
twice until the answer arrives:

{@ocaml[
# let tags = Tags.make [ "bug" ] |> Tags.apply ~id:4 (Add "ui");;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# let tags = Tags.rebase [ "bug"; "ui" ] tags;;
val tags : Tags.t = <abstr>
# Tags.value tags, Tags.pending tags;;
- : Tags.value * (int * change) list = (["bug"; "ui"], [(4, Add "ui")])
# Tags.value (Tags.settle ~id:4 tags);;
- : Tags.value = ["bug"; "ui"]
]}

A change that sets a field to a value is idempotent already. Give the
checker a property such as "an item never shows twice" and samples where the
published state holds the change: it finds an [apply] that is not
idempotent.

Mini Linear edits the fields of an issue in this way. Each change goes to the
server in its own Remote call, made by a small child component, and a failed
change shows a toast. See {!page-linear.features}.
